# The easiest way to put HTTPS in front — Caddy.
#
# Important: once people start **logging in with accounts**, HTTPS is effectively required.
#    Over plain http, passwords and login cookies travel in the clear, and anyone on the same network
#    can pick them up. The internal network is no exception.
#
# Usage
#   cp Caddyfile.example Caddyfile      # change the domain to your own
#   caddy run --config Caddyfile
#
# Caddy obtains and renews the certificate on its own (for a public domain).
# Details: docs/설치.md "Opening to the internet" · docs/사용법.md §12-3

aas.사내도메인 {
	# If an internal domain cannot get a public certificate, uncomment this line.
	# It is self-signed, so the Caddy root certificate must be installed once on in-house PCs
	#   caddy trust
	# tls internal

	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
}

# Set these to match (.env)
#   AAS_PUBLIC_URL=https://aas.사내도메인   the address the registry announces to other systems
#
# Also bind the port in docker-compose.yml so nothing gets in without going through the proxy
#   ports: ["127.0.0.1:${APP_PORT:-8080}:8080"]
#
# The program reads the X-Forwarded-Proto that Caddy adds and **only over HTTPS**
# sets Secure on the login cookie and sends HSTS — if a plain-http server sent HSTS,
# browsers would lock that address to https-only for good.
